ravi: FYI — EdgeWorks sent today's usage dump and our billing shows ~3.2k token-equivalent overcharge for CX-724. Looks like a unit mismatch. Posting a tiny example from their feed so we can reason about mapping.

ana: Ack. Do we know if they're on the v2 partner schema or still on the old v1 flat CSV?

ravi: They replied 'v2 JSON' in the email. Example payload they sent:```json
{
  "customer_id": "C-123",
  "usage_events": [
    {"unit_type": "active_unit", "quantity": 24, "start_ts": 1710000000, "end_ts": 1710003600},
    {"unit_type": "active_unit", "quantity": 30, "start_ts": 1710003600, "end_ts": 1710007200}
  ],
  "report_window": "hour"
}
```

samir: I think the root cause is the semantic: EdgeWorks measures concurrent sessions (active_unit) while our marketplace SKU expects licensed seats (monthly). Active_unit is an ephemeral concurrency metric — we can't 1:1 bill that as seats.

liz: Can we ask them to include an entitlement_id and aggregation_grain for mapping? Our ingestion pipeline relies on entitlement_id to map to the customer's contracted SKU. If it's missing we fall back to fuzzy sku mapping which is error-prone.

mike-sales: Client is on SI-managed onboarding and EdgeWorks is resistant to changing the feed format mid-quarter. We need a short-term mapping strategy so billing isn't blocked. Any ideas?

samir: Short-term: create an ingest transformer that converts active_unit events into seat-minutes (quantity * window_seconds / 60) and then roll up to licensed-seat-equivalents by dividing by minutes-per-seat (e.g., 60*24*30). But this is an approximation and must be marked provisional in the ledger.

ravi: I'll prototype that transform in partner-ingest. Example transform pseudocode:```js
// convert active_unit (concurrent sessions) to seat-minutes
for (evt of usage_events) {
  const windowSec = evt.end_ts - evt.start_ts;
  const seatMinutes = evt.quantity * (windowSec / 60);
  emit({ customer_id, entitlement_hint: "active_unit->seat_minute", amount: seatMinutes, denom: "seat_minutes" });
}
```

devbot: reconcile-job created: rj-8423 for C-123 (backfill + provisional credits)

ana: Ops plan: 1) Ask EdgeWorks to add entitlement_id + aggregation_grain in next feed. 2) Deploy ingest transform as temporary adapter with a visible flag in the console (so finance sees provisional mapping). 3) Run a 7-day backfill and surface suspected overbills as provisional credits. Who owns partner comms?

mike-sales: I'll own comms with EdgeWorks and push for a schema change. I'll also ask for a sample week of raw telemetry so Ravi can validate the transform. ETA by EOD.

samir: I'll add a ledger-bridge rule to apply provisional credits and tag them with source:edgeworks-ingest-transform. We'll avoid auto-collection on those amounts until partner confirms entitlement mapping.

liz: Also adding this to the partnerships onboarding checklist: require explicit aggregation_grain and entitlement_id, plus a mapping example for concurrency-based meters.

ravi: Thanks. I'll drop the spike branch and a sample webhook payload into the integration PR. Posting back here when done.

mike-sales: Pinged EdgeWorks — they're open to adding entitlement_id but asked for the transform approach as a stopgap. Will update with their sample telemetry. :thumbsup:

ana: Great. TL;DR: temporary transform -> provisional credits -> partner schema change -> finalize reconciliation. Keep ops in the loop.

ravi: Cool, will follow up.
